      "answer": {
        "coverage": "complete",
        "insufficient_evidence": false,
        "summary": "Tolkin recommends framing reviews as intellectual conversations aimed primarily at improving the product, while leaders probe with curiosity, clearly label ideas as suggestions rather than mandates, and recognize the team’s deeper problem context. Keep the discussion small and share its artifacts more broadly.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2",
          "S5"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Set a constructive purpose",
            "content": "Be explicit that reviews serve accountability and informing an audience, but make improving the product the primary goal. Aim for an intellectual conversation about the work that helps the team think through the problem—not a “firing squad.” Tolkin notes that discussions may sometimes get intense, but that should not be the general atmosphere.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S2"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Lead through questions and context, not mandates",
            "content": "Tolkin says the people closest to a problem often have the best context to solve it. As a senior leader, probe, ask questions, and offer ideas while making clear they are thoughts, not mandates. When you have relevant missing context, share it directly rather than disguising it as a question. Push on dimensions the team may not be considering, while recognizing that you bring breadth and the team brings depth: they may spend 40–60 hours a week on the problem while you spend three.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Keep the discussion focused and the record accessible",
            "content": "Keep the live conversation relatively small—Tolkin says they try to stay under 10 people—while distributing the resulting document broadly. The record can be a document or a recording, depending on company culture; it can also help new hires understand prior product discussions.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S5"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "missing_topics": []
      },
